- Reuse styling from globalStyles:
```
import globalStyles from "./index.css?inline";
```

- Design each component to have a single, well-defined responsibility.
Keep render() pure by avoiding side effects and asynchronous work.
- Expose only your public API with @property() and keep implementation details in @state().
- Prefer property bindings (.value) over attributes for objects, arrays, and functions.
- Pass data down through properties and communicate changes up with custom events.
- Use Lit directives like repeat(), when(), cache(), and classMap() instead of manual template logic.
- Use repeat() with stable keys when rendering dynamic lists.
- Use the appropriate lifecycle hook (firstUpdated(), updated(), connectedCallback(), etc.) for each type of work.
- Build accessibility in from the start with semantic HTML, keyboard support, and ARIA where needed.
- Expose styling through CSS custom properties, ::part, and slots instead of relying on internal DOM structure.
- Prefer immutable state updates over mutating objects or arrays in place.
- Keep asynchronous operations out of render() and perform them in lifecycle methods, controllers, or dedicated tasks.
- Extract shared behavior into Reactive Controllers instead of inheritance or mixins.
- Write components in TypeScript to improve type safety and developer experience.
- Publish standard ES modules with minimal bundling to maximize compatibility.
- Minimize public API surface area to make components easier to understand and maintain.
- Favor composition of small components over large, monolithic components.
- Clean up event listeners, observers, and subscriptions in disconnectedCallback().
- Use native HTML elements whenever they meet your accessibility and functionality needs.
- Write components to be framework-agnostic by relying on web standards rather than framework-specific patterns.